# inc-18q6 phase 2: passed-save entanglement must penalize, not disable.
# Frozen monsters keep the action oracle about the player's own actions.
# Paint two adjacent squares: tanglefoot rolls on departure and on strikes.
#
# THE SUBJECT is the orc rogue of chargen-rogue.keys, not the human paladin
# check_entangle_escape.sh keeps. A paladin's Reflex save is bad enough that
# a chain of independent DC 15 rolls (tanglefoot re-rolls that save on every
# move AND every strike from the square, lib/alchemy.irh) almost never lines
# up two, three, four passes in a row -- seeds 1 to 10 all failed on him. A
# rogue's good Reflex progression plus his Dexterity gives him a real bonus
# on this save, so a bounded chain of retries has a real chance of finishing
# instead of measuring dice. On seed 4 that bonus is Reflex +5, a 55% chance
# per independent roll.
#
# ORDER: the attack test runs BEFORE the movement test, on purpose. Both
# tests need the subject standing on a tanglefoot square and Entangled, not
# Stuck, at the moment they are graded, and both need retries: a failed save
# Sticks the subject instead of granting the roll being tested, and each
# retry is itself a fresh independent roll. An attack retry can never move
# the subject -- attacking never changes his square -- so running the
# attack loop first is free of risk. A move retry that SUCCEEDS, by
# contrast, walks him one square closer to open floor; run enough of those
# in a row (only two tanglefoot squares are painted) and he leaves the
# hazard entirely. Running the movement test last, after the attack test,
# means that overshoot is not a defect: this fixture WANTS him to walk
# clear of the hazard by the end of the move loop, for the reason below.
# An earlier version of this fixture ran the movement test first and
# calibrated the exact number of attempts to stop at, to avoid overshoot on
# one seed's exact dice -- that is what the Part A task brief calls
# calibration rather than a fixture, and it is why this version does not do
# that.
#
# WHY THE FLOOR BASELINE IS TAKEN LAST, NOT FIRST. A rogue starts Hiding,
# which is its own movement penalty ("50% hiding" on the Movement Rate
# line), and combat ends it -- measured while building this fixture: every
# "before"/"moved" screen from the attack loop onward shows no Hiding tag,
# where every screen before combat still shows one. Measuring the ordinary
# floor cost before the fight and the entangled cost after it would compare
# two different subjects: one paying a hiding penalty the other one is not.
# So this fixture pays the entangled cost FIRST (from the same fight-worn,
# no-longer-hiding subject) and measures the floor cost SECOND, once the
# move loop has walked him back to ordinary ground -- both halves of the
# comparison are the one post-combat subject.
#
# THE MOVE RETRY needs no separate recovery step. A failed attempt leaves
# the subject Stuck rather than moved, and the very next LEFT keypress --
# sent by this same loop, nothing extra needed -- is read by
# Creature::Walk as an escape attempt while Stuck, so the loop naturally
# alternates between "try to leave" and "try to tear free" without a
# separate recovery block. tools/check_entangled_acts.sh finds the FIRST
# attempt that actually left the square while Entangled and not Stuck, and
# grades that one and no other; the loop then keeps going (it does not stop
# there) so that by the last attempt the subject has walked off BOTH
# tanglefoot squares, clearing the ground the trailing floor baseline needs.
#
# THE ATTACK RETRY does need a recovery step: PRE(EV_STRIKE) sits on the
# same event list as EV_MOVE (lib/alchemy.irh) and re-rolls the same Reflex
# DC 15 on a strike from the square, same as leaving it would -- but only
# while the subject is Entangled, not Stuck; an attack thrown while already
# Stuck does not re-roll it (measured while building this fixture: attacks
# in a row from Stuck printed no "Reflex Save:" line at all, and Stuck
# never lifted on its own). So after a strike leaves him Stuck, the script
# spends "@while "Stuck" LEFT" -- Strength/Escape Artist checks,
# src/Move.cpp -- until he tears free, and it must stop the moment Stuck
# lifts, because one keypress too many would actually walk him off the
# square this fixture is measuring. Escaping never moves him (it only ever
# clears Stuck while he stands on the square he got stuck on), so this
# cannot overshoot the way a move retry can. @while is capped at 200 passes
# (src/Wposix.cpp SK_LOOP_MAX) and gives up rather than hang, so this cannot
# loop forever even on a subject whose escape chance is poor.
#
# THE DISENGAGE PROMPT. Once the summoned troll turns hostile, walking
# away from it while adjacent asks "Abort, Flee or Disengage?" (src/Move.cpp)
# -- a different prompt from the hazard-entry "Confirm" one, and it blocks
# the run if nothing answers it. "f" (Flee) always completes the move at
# the cost of one attack of opportunity, which is what every LEFT in the
# move loop needs: an aborted move would print no violation but would also
# never satisfy "the subject moved," stalling the loop for nothing.
#
# tools/check_entangled_acts.sh finds the first attack attempt that lands a
# printed "Attack:" line while Entangled and not Stuck, and grades that
# one, and the first move attempt that actually left the square while
# Entangled and not Stuck, and grades that one. Ten attempts of each; if
# all ten of either miss, the check reports INCONCLUSIVE rather than FAIL,
# because that is a measurement failure, not a proven defect.
#
# The "Stuck"/"Entangled" checks below match bare words, not anchored to
# the pipe that ends the status field ("... Stuck |" or "... Entangled |"):
# a third status (Hiding, here) can sit between one of these and the pipe,
# so an anchored pattern silently never matches for Stuck. Entangled is
# always the last status word the game prints before the pipe on this
# subject, so anchoring it to " |" is safe and used for the one loop that
# needs to stop AT Entangled rather than merely detect it.
#
# A TROLL takes the swing, not the paladin fixture's bugbear: measured
# while building this version, a rogue's sneak attack (+1d8 while hidden,
# unavailable to a paladin) plus ten un-recovering swings killed the
# bugbear on some seeds partway through the attack loop -- and once the
# target is dead, the next "UP" keypress the loop sends is read as a plain
# move instead of an attack, silently walking the subject off the hazard
# square before the move loop even starts. A troll regenerates 5 hit
# points a round, which no amount of ordinary weapon damage from this
# subject outpaces, so it cannot die mid-loop; a babau was tried and
# rejected for the same reason the paladin version rejected it -- its acid
# touch corrupts the same "Attack:" line the oracle reads and can melt the
# subject's weapon outright. Measured HP cost to the subject across ten
# attack attempts plus the escape rounds that follow: at most 6 of 33, no
# death on any seed tried. While STUCK a weapon attack can still land
# (Phase 3, R7) without clearing the Stuck the same swing was blocked by
# moments before -- which is exactly why the checker requires Entangled and
# not Stuck at the moment of the hit, not merely a hit.
#
# The checker tracks the player's own column on screen, run to run within
# this one session: the display does not scroll over the handful of
# squares this fixture ever moves across, so the raw column of '@' is a
# stable, landmark-free position signal. (The paladin version anchored on
# a '#' wall visible from the entry chamber; this room, generated after a
# different amount of chargen dice, never shows one within view.)
@include chargen-rogue.keys
@until "Afoot" ESC
w
y
@choose "Wizard Mode Switches"
DOWN*10
SPACE
ESC
d
@dump:baseline
ESC
